## 10. What this fix does NOT prove

* **It does not prove the seal is authentic.** There is no key or MAC, so anyone who can write the
  log can re-seal it and get exit 0 (§5.3). The checker detects accident and careless tampering, not
  a determined forger. Only an HMAC (key held outside the attempt directory) would change that.
* **It does not prove the attempt is unmodified.** Only `stages/*` and the log prefix are covered.
  `child-evidence/**`, `$RUN/option-c/**`, and files the plan claims but never wrote
  (`toolchain-resolution.txt`) are outside the seal. Moving the whole directory elsewhere still
  yields exit 0, because nothing binds the seal to its location.
* **It does not prove the byte-length is pinned.** The accepted shape is self-consistent, not
  absolute: removing the marker's terminating LF passes (§5.1), and when that LF is absent the byte
  assertion is blind to appends and only the sha layer saves it (§5.2).
* **It does not prove every trailing byte was caught by the byte gate.** For the no-LF shape the
  gate can be inert; the "appends always fail" property rests on the sha comparison, not on the new
  byte assertion. Keep the sha layer.
* **It does not prove the batch exercise tested the new capability.** Its own summary says so, and I
  confirm it: no real attempt carries the byte-level tamper, so the batch demonstrates zero
  regression only (`evidence/xc2-batch-summary.txt` shows both checkers differing on 0 directories).
  The new capability rests on synthetic controls.
* **Not verified (environment/wording scope):** only GNU userland was exercised (bash 5.3.9, grep
  3.12, coreutils 9.11); behaviour under BSD/busybox `tail -c +N` / `grep -b` semantics was **not**
  tested (blocker: no such userland in this environment). Whether any *out-of-tree* consumer parses
  the changed failure text was only checked in-repo (none found; `selftest.sh` uses rc only).
* **Not verified:** `README.md`'s unchanged-ness against an independent pre-change copy (§1).
